<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mehmed IV Giray</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1674">1674</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giray-dynasty">Giray dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mehmed IV Giray was khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1641–1644 and again in 1654–1666. He supported Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> against Muscovy in the Russo-Polish War, and in 1659 his cavalry helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">Hetman</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-vyhovsky">Ivan Vyhovsky</a> defeat a Muscovite army at Konotop. Deposed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1666, he ended his life in exile.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mehmed, called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a>, was a son of Khan Selâmet I Giray and had some reputation as a poet. From 1632 to 1635 he served as nureddin or kalga during the second reign of his cousin Canibek Giray, and he then went to Ottoman territory, probably with the deposed Canibek. Nothing seems to be recorded of his life under the khans İnayet Giray and his brother Bahadır I Giray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mehmed came to the throne in 1641 on the death of Bahadır. In 1642 Crimean and Turkish troops, accompanied by the traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a>, drove the Don Cossacks out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a>, which Bahadır had tried and failed to do. His brother Islyam complained to the sultan that he had the better claim, and when the Turkish governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a> reported that Mehmed had ravaged Circassia without authority, the khan was deposed and replaced by Islyam. Mehmed was living at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> when Islyam died, and he returned to the throne in 1654.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had just allied with the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> switched sides to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the Russo-Polish War of 1654 to 1667, though the alliance was loose and Poland paid the Crimeans to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In 1657 the Crimeans attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-rakoczi">George II Rákóczi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, and the next year they sacked his capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-iulia">Gyulafehérvár</a>, and returned with about 17,000 slaves. In 1660 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Crimeans defeated Russia at the Battle of Chudnov, and in 1661 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> defeated John Kemény in Transylvania.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1666 Mehmed was deposed for sending his son instead of going himself to fight in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and for attacking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nogais">Nogais</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>. He retired to the Shamkhalate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarki">Tarki</a>, where he was granted an estate and lived as a dervish, and his ashes were returned to Bakhchisarai for burial. None of his sons or grandsons became khan. His wife Emine Sultan Biyim is one of only three women known to have had a political role in the Crimean Khanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mehmed IV Giray” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Eastern Europe: Islam III Giray allied with the Cossack hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky in 1648, contributing to victories at Zhovti Vody and Korsun before abandoning him at Berestechko in 1651, and <b>Mehmed IV Giray</b> helped Hetman Ivan Vyhovsky defeat a Russian army at the Battle of Konotop in 1659.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
